{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Let me plan step 2.\n\nRequirements:\n1. Implement the checker in linkcheck.py (standard library only):\n   - Discover .md files under a directory\n   - Extract inline links\n   - Report broken local file links as `file:line: target`\n   - Exit 1 if there are any broken links\n2. Add a helper `heading_slug(text)` that converts a heading into a GitHub-style anchor slug, defined right above the `if __name__ == '__main__':` guard\n3. Leave the docstring at the top\n4. Create docs/ containing three small sample .md files that link to each other, with one link broken\n5. Run it\n\nDesign:\n\n- File discovery: if a file/directory is given as an argument, use it as-is. If no argument, scan the current directory recursively for *.md, skipping .git, node_modules, vendor.\n- Link extraction: regex for inline links `[text](target)` — but be careful about images `![alt](img.png)` (these are also valid local file links and should be checked). Autolinks `<file.md>` — the docstring says we check them, but the step says \"extract inline links\". Keep the scope narrow: inline links `[text](target)` (including image `![alt](...)`). Maybe autolinks too, since the docstring mentions them. The step says \"extract inline links\" — I'll go with inline links including images, and autolinks since they're documented. Actually, let's keep it simple and robust: the main regex for `[text](target)` will match image links too (since it matches `![alt](...)` as well). Let's write the regex:\n\n```python\nLINK_RE = re.compile(r'(?<!\\!)\\[([^\\]]*)\\]\\(([^)\\s]+)(?:\\s+\"[^\"]*\")?\\)')\n```\n\nHmm, but I want to include images too. More simply: `\\[([^\\]]*)\\]\\(([^)]+)\\)` and parse the target. Strip the title part ` \"title\"`.\n\nTarget classification:\n- Skip http://, https://, mailto:, tel:, data: (unless --check-remote; in step 2, skip remote)\n- Anchors-only `#foo` — target is the file itself. Skip or check the anchor? In step 2, we'll do local file links. `#anchor` — the target is the same file, which exists. We could check the anchor against headings, but step 2 only says \"report broken local file links\". Keep it simple: for `#anchor`, resolve to the same file (always exists) — skip.\n- Relative path: resolve against the file's directory (or --base-dir). Check existence with os.path.exists.\n- Strip fragment: `path#anchor` → check existence of path; in step 2 skip anchor checking (heading_slug is just a helper for later).\n\nActually, heading_slug needs to be defined right above the guard. In step 2 it might be unused, but that's fine — it's a helper for later steps. I could use it lightly… the requirement says to add the helper, defined right above the guard. OK.\n\nGitHub-style slug algorithm:\n- Lowercase\n- Remove anything other than word characters (letters, digits, hyphens, underscores, spaces)\n- Replace spaces with hyphens\n- (Newer GitHub keeps apostrophes etc. but strips punctuation; classic algorithm: lowercase, strip punctuation, spaces → hyphens)\n\n```python\ndef heading_slug(text):\n    \"\"\"Turn a heading into a GitHub-style anchor slug.\"\"\"\n    slug = text.strip().lower()\n    slug = re.sub(r'[^\\w\\s-]', '', slug)  # strip punctuation\n    slug = re.sub(r'\\s+', '-', slug)\n    return slug\n```\n\nOrder: GitHub: strip punctuation (except spaces and hyphens), lowercase, spaces→hyphens. My implementation above: strip leading/trailing whitespace, lowercase, remove characters other than word chars/space/hyphen, collapse whitespace into hyphens. That's close enough. Note that `\\w` includes underscores and digits, no problem.\n\nEdge case: `re.sub(r'\\s+', '-', slug)` — what about leading/trailing spaces after punctuation removal? E.g. \"Hello, World!\" → \"hello world\" → \"hello-world\". Good. \"C++ Tips\" → \"c tips\" → \"c-tips\". GitHub produces \"c-tips\". Good.\n\nOutput format: `file:line: target`. Example: `docs/broken.md:4: ../missing.md`.\n\nExit codes: 0 all OK, 1 any broken, 2 usage error (per docstring: \"no files found\" → 2).\n\nCLI: the docstring promises --check-remote, --allow-missing, --base-dir, --verbose. In step 2, implement what's needed: FILES argument, maybe --base-dir and --verbose. --check-remote and --allow-missing can be deferred. argparse with these options but --check-remote/--allow-missing as stubs (accepted but noted as unimplemented)? Hmm, better to keep step 2 narrow: the requirement is \"discover .md files under a directory, extract inline links, report broken local file links as file:line: target, exit 1 if any\".\n\nWhat to implement:\n- positional FILES (nargs='*'): files or directories\n- --base-dir DIR\n- --verbose\n- --check-remote and --allow-missing: accepted (argparse) but in step 2 just a no-op with a \"not implemented yet\" note? That would be misleading. Alternative: implement --allow-missing simply (report but exit 0) — it's trivial, so implement it. --check-remote: accept but in step 2 skip remote links anyway, so if the flag is given, just emit a \"not implemented\" warning to stderr. Hmm. Simplest: implement --allow-missing (2 lines), and for --check-remote emit a stderr warning that it's not implemented in this step. Actually, to keep the code honest and minimal, I'll implement --allow-missing and emit a stderr warning for --check-remote that it's a no-op in this step. This keeps the docstring truthful enough. Or just omit both options from argparse for now… but the docstring documents them. argparse without them would break `python linkcheck.py --check-remote` from the docstring examples. I'll include them with minimal behavior.\n\nLink extraction details:\n- Line-by-line scan with regex. Handle `]` inside text? Keep it simple: `[^\\]]*`.\n- Target may contain a title: `(target \"title\")` — strip it.\n- Code blocks: links inside fenced code blocks (``` … ```) should be skipped. This is important for accuracy. Track fences.\n- Skip links inside inline code spans? Overkill for