{"kind":"delta","request":6925,"seq":8167,"t":1053487,"tool_call":{"arguments":"{\"file_path\": \"linkcheck.py\", \"new_string\": \"for m in LINK_RE.finditer(line):\\n            yield lineno, m.group(1)\\n\\n\\ndef collect_headings(text):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return the set of GitHub-style slugs for ATX headings in text.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    slugs = set()\\n    in_fence = False\\n    for line in text.splitlines():\\n        if FENCE_RE.match(line):\\n            in_fence = not in_fence\\n            continue\\n        if in_fence:\\n            continue\\n        m = ATX_RE.match(line)\\n        if not m:\\n            continue\\n        heading = re.sub(r\\\"\\\\s+#+\\\\s*$\\\", \\\"\\\", m.group(1)).strip()\\n        if heading:\\n            slugs.add(heading_slug(heading))\\n    return slugs\\n\\n\\ndef check_file(path, base_dir):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Check one file.\\n\\n    Returns a list of (line_number, target, status) for local links,\\n    where status is \\\"ok\\\", \\\"missing\\\", or \\\"noanchor\\\"; or None if the\\n    file cannot be read.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    try:\\n        with open(path, encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") as f:\\n            text = f.read()\\n    except OSError as e:\\n        print(f\\\"error: {path}: {e}\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n        return None\\n\\n    base = base_dir or os.path.dirname(path)\\n    results = []\\n    for lineno, target in extract_links(text):\\n        if SCHEME_RE.match(target):\\n            continue  # remote/mailto: not checked in this step\\n        path_part, _, fragment = target.partition(\\\"#\\\")\\n        path_part = urllib.parse.unquote(path_part)\\n        fragment = urllib.parse.unquote(fragment)\\n        target_path = path if not path_part else os.path.join(base, path_part)\\n        if not os.path.exists(target_path):\\n            results.append((lineno, target, \\\"missing\\\"))\\n            continue\\n        if not fragment:\\n            results.append((lineno, target, \\\"ok\\\"))\\n            continue\\n        try:\\n            with open(target_path, encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") as f:\\n                headings = collect_headings(f.read())\\n        except OSError:\\n            results.append((lineno, target, \\\"ok\\\"))  # skip anchor check\\n            continue\\n        status = \\\"ok\\\" if heading_slug(fragment) in headings else \\\"noanchor\\\"\\n        results.append((lineno, target, status))\\n    return results\", \"old_string\": \"for m in LINK_RE.finditer(line):\\n            yield lineno, m.group(1)\\n\\n\\ndef check_file(path, base_dir):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Check one file.\\n\\n    Returns a list of (line_number, target, ok) for local links,\\n    or None if the file cannot be read.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    try:\\n        with open(path, encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") as f:\\n            text = f.read()\\n    except OSError as e:\\n        print(f\\\"error: {path}: {e}\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n        return None\\n\\n    base = base_dir or os.path.dirname(path)\\n    results = []\\n    for lineno, target in extract_links(text):\\n        if SCHEME_RE.match(target):\\n            continue  # remote/mailto: not checked in this step\\n        path_part = target.split(\\\"#\\\", 1)[0]\\n        if not path_part:\\n            continue  # pure in-page anchor\\n        ok = os.path.exists(os.path.join(base, path_part))\\n        results.append((lineno, target, ok))\\n    return results\"}","id":"call_f18055d7c7664ed091e4cf44","index":3,"name":"edit"}}
{"kind":"delta","request":6925,"seq":8168,"t":1053487,"tool_call":{"arguments":"{\"file_path\": \"linkcheck.py\", \"new_string\": \"broken = []\\n    for path in files:\\n        results = check_file(path, args.base_dir)\\n        if results is None:\\n            return 2\\n        for lineno, target, status in results:\\n            if status == \\\"ok\\\":\\n                if args.verbose:\\n                    print(f\\\"{path}:{lineno}: {target} (ok)\\\")\\n            else:\\n                broken.append((path, lineno, target, status))\\n\\n    for path, lineno, target, status in broken:\\n        suffix = \\\" (NOANCHOR)\\\" if status == \\\"noanchor\\\" else \\\"\\\"\\n        print(f\\\"{path}:{lineno}: {target}{suffix}\\\")\\n    return 1 if broken and not args.allow_missing else 0\", \"old_string\": \"broken = []\\n    for path in files:\\n        results = check_file(path, args.base_dir)\\n        if results is None:\\n            return 2\\n        for lineno, target, ok in results:\\n            if ok:\\n                if args.verbose:\\n                    print(f\\\"{path}:{lineno}: {target} (ok)\\\")\\n            else:\\n                broken.append((path, lineno, target))\\n\\n    for path, lineno, target in broken:\\n        print(f\\\"{path}:{lineno}: {target}\\\")\\n    return 1 if broken and not args.allow_missing else 0\"}","id":"call_37bd8f6217974fcc96b2f3c5","index":4,"name":"edit"}}
{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Scope: local links only. Add `#anchor` checks: links like `other.md#some-heading` must match a heading in the target file using `heading_slug`. Pure `#anchor` links should also be checked against the same file (the docstring states that anchors are checked against ATX headings — the docstring says `NOANCHOR` report for existing targets where the anchor cannot be found).\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n1. A function to collect headings from Markdown text: ATX headings (`#` through `######` at the start of a line, followed by a space and text). Should headings inside code fences be skipped? Ideally yes — use fence tracking similar to extract_links.\n\n2. `collect_headings(text)` → list of slugs (using heading_slug). Should it be a dict for duplicates? GitHub handles duplicates by appending `-1`, `-2`... For step 3, keep it simple: set of slugs. Duplicates: the first one wins, or allow either. Use a set.\n\n3. In `check_file`, for each local link with a fragment: check that the slug is in the target file's headings (or in the same file's case if there's no path part). Report format: the docstring says \"targets that exist but whose anchor cannot be found are reported as `NOANCHOR`\". Output format requirement: broken links are `file:line: target`. For NOANCHOR, output `file:line: target (NOANCHOR)`? The user said \"keep the `file:line: target` output, exit 1 if anything is broken\".\n\nHmm. Keep it simple: broken = missing file OR missing anchor. Output as `file:line: target`. Should we add a marker? The docstring mentions the `NOANCHOR` status. But the user explicitly said \"keep the `file:line: target` output\". To satisfy both, we could output `file:line: target (NOANCHOR)` for anchor failures — that's an extension of the format. Or keep the exact `file:line: target` for both. The user's instruction is explicit: keep the `file:line: target` output. I'll keep the exact format for missing files, and add a `(NOANCHOR)` suffix for anchor failures — no wait, that changes the format.\n\nLet me re-read: \"keep the `file:line: target` output, exit 1 if anything is broken. Add `#anchor` checks: links like other.md#some-heading must match a heading in the target, using heading_slug.\"\n\nSafest interpretation: keep the same output format for broken links (missing file), and anchor failures are also broken → same format. But then you can't distinguish missing file from missing anchor. The docstring promised NOANCHOR. I think outputting `file:line: target (NOANCHOR)` is a reasonable and minimal extension that maintains readability. But strictly, \"keep the file:line: target output\" — the anchor failure case is new, and its format isn't specified. I'll use `file:line: target (NOANCHOR)` for anchor failures and keep plain `file:line: target` for missing files. That's defensible and documented.\n\nActually, to be minimal and safe: keep both as `file:line: target`? Then the output is indistinguishable. Hmm. I'll go with the `(NOANCHOR)` suffix — it matches the status name in the docstring and is a small, clear extension. I'll mention it in the reply so the user can reject it.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n```python\ndef collect_headings(text):\n    \"\"\"Return the set of GitHub-style slugs for ATX headings in text.\"\"\"\n    slugs = set()\n    in_fence = False\n    for line in text.splitlines():\n        if FENCE_RE.match(line):\n            in_fence = not in_fence\n            continue\n        if in_fence:\n            continue\n        m = re.match(r\"^(#{1,6})\\s+(.*\\S)\\s*#*\\s*$\", line)\n        if m:\n            slugs.add(heading_slug(m.group(2)))\n    return slugs\n```\n\nATX heading regex: `^#{1,6}\\s+...` — headings require a space after the hashes (in most flavors, `#Heading` without a space is not a heading; CommonMark requires a space or end of line). Trailing `#` closing sequence: `## Foo ##` → text is \"Foo\". Handle that.\n\nNote: heading_slug is defined in the file *after* check_file but before the guard — calling it from check_file is fine since it's resolved at runtime.\n\nChanges to check_file:\n\n```python\ndef check_file(path, base_dir):\n    try:\n        with open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n            text = f.read()\n    except OSError as e:\n        print(f\"error: {path}: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return None\n\n    base = base_dir or os.path.dirname(path)\n    results = []\n    for lineno, target in extract_links(text):\n        if SCHEME_RE.match(target):\n            continue\n        path_part, sep, fragment = target.partition(\"#\")\n        if not path_part:\n            target_path = path\n        else:\n            target_path = os.path.join(base, path_part)\n        if not os.path.exists(target_path):\n            results.append((lineno, target, \"missing\"))\n            continue\n        if not fragment:\n            results.append((lineno, target, \"ok\"))\n            continue\n        try:\n            with open(target_path, encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n                headings = collect_headings(f.read())\n        except OSError:\n            results.append((lineno, target, \"ok\"))  # can't read target; skip anchor check\n            continue\n        if heading_slug(fragment) in headings:\n            results.append((lineno, target, \"ok\"))\n        else:\n            results.append((lineno, target, \"noanchor\"))\n    return results\n```\n\nWait — the fragment inside the link might already be a slug, or it might be raw. GitHub compares the link's fragment (URL-decoded) against the slug. The link's fragment is usually already a slug (e.g., `#some-heading`). Compare `heading_slug(fragment)` against the slug — but applying heading_slug to an already-slugified fragment is mostly idempotent (lowercase, strip punctuation, spaces→hyphens). If the fragment contains a space, heading_slug will turn it into a hyphen — that's actually useful. But if the fragment is `#some_heading`, slug → `some_heading`, which matches the heading slug of \"some_heading\" or \"Some heading\"? Heading \"Some heading\" → slug \"some-heading\", not \"some_heading\". GitHub would produce the same mismatch. Fine.\n\nHmm, one subtle point: URL-encoding of fragments (`%20`). For simplicity, skip unquoting? `urllib.parse.unquote` would handle `%20`. A small addition: use unquote on the fragment before slugifying. Let's do it — one import (urllib.parse) and one call. Actually, I hadn't imported urllib before. Adding it is fine (stdlib). Or skip it. I'll add it — it's cheap and correct.\n\nHmm, wait, also URL-decoding of path_part: `foo%20bar.md`. Let's unquote both. Keep it minimal: unquote path_part and fragment.\n\nNow results become 3-tuples with a status string: \"ok\", \"missing\", \"noanchor\". Update main:\n\n```python\n    broken = []\n    for path in files:\n        results = check_file(path, args.base_dir)\n        if results is None:\n            return 2\n        for lineno, target, status in results:\n            if status == \"ok\":\n                if args.verbose:\n                    print(f\"{path}:{lineno}: {target} (ok)\")\n            elif status == \"noanchor\":\n                broken.append((path, lineno, target, \"noanchor\"))\n            else:\n                broken.append((path, lineno, target, \"missing\"))\n\n    for path, lineno, target, status in broken:\n        if status == \"noanchor\":\n            print(f\"{path}:{lineno}: {target} (NOANCHOR)\")\n        else:\n            print(f\"{path}:{lineno}: {target}\")\n    return 1 if broken and not args.allow_missing else 0\n```\n\nAlso update the docstring: the \"Supported link forms\" section already says anchors are checked against ATX headings, and NOANCHOR is mentioned. The docstring said \"Autolinks `<file.md>` are checked as file targets\" — that's not implemented. And \"Image links are checked as file targets\" — images are matched by LINK_RE (yes, `\\[[^\\]]*\\]` also matches `![alt]`? Let me ch