{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Now I need to add the following:\n\n1. **Reference-style links**: `[text][ref]` with `[ref]: target` definitions. Also, the shorthand `[ref]` where the label matches the reference. Need to collect definitions and resolve references.\n\n2. **`--json` flag**: Output broken links as a JSON list instead of lines.\n\nLet me think about reference-style link semantics (CommonMark):\n\n- Reference definition: `[label]: destination` (with optional title). A definition is only a definition when at the start of a line (after up to 3 spaces of indent).\n- Reference link: `[text][label]` — resolves label (case-insensitive) to the destination of the definition.\n- Shorthand reference link: `[label]` — text == label.\n- Collapsed form: `[text][]` — resolves `text` as the label.\n\nHow does reference-link checking work?\n\n- `extract_links` should also yield reference links: find `[text][label]` and `[label]` (shorthand) where the label has a definition.\n- `[text]` without a definition is not a link — should not be reported.\n- Definitions themselves: `[ref]: target` — do we check the target? The definition's target is only used when referenced. I think the simplest approach is: collect definitions (label → target, line), and check links when they're used. If a definition is never referenced, it's not a link — don't check it. Actually, many linkcheckers check the target of definitions too, but... the spec says \"reference-style links ([text][ref] with [ref]: target definitions)\". So links are what get checked.\n\nBut wait — should an unreferenced definition be reported? In a typical markdown lint, no — only links that get rendered are checked. I'll only check referenced ones. Hmm, but what about a definition with a missing target that's never referenced? That doesn't produce a link, so don't check it. That's the CommonMark-correct behavior.\n\nAlso, note: reference definitions appear as `[label]: target` at the start of