exclude definition lines from reference-link scanning. Also, `[text][ref]` could appear on a definition line? Not really, but excluding definition lines is safe.\n\nAlso, inline code spans: `[text](...)` inside backticks. The existing tool doesn't handle that; I'll keep consistency (not handle code spans).\n\nLet me design the regexes:\n\nREF_DEF_RE = `^\\s{0,3}\\[([^\\]]+)\\]:\\s*(\\S+)(?:\\s+\"[^\"]*\")?\\s*$`\n\nReference link candidates:\n- Full form: `\\[(text)\\]\\[label\\]` → `\\[((?:[^\\[\\]]|...))\\]\\[([^\\]]*)\\]` — keep it simple: `\\[[^\\]]*\\]\\[([^\\]]*)\\]` with group 1 = text, group 2 = label. Collapsed: label is empty → use text.\n- Shorthand: `\\[[^\\]]*\\]` not followed by `(`, `]`, or `:`. Need to avoid matching:\n  - `[text](target)` — inline link (already handled; would the shorthand regex match `[text]` and then check if `[text]` is a defined label? In CommonMark, `[text](target)` is an inline link; `[text]` on its own isn't. But if `[text]` is a defined label... in CommonMark, `[text](target)` is always an inline link, not a reference link. So I must ensure the shorthand doesn't match when `[label]` is followed by `(` or `[`.\n  - `[label]: target` — definition line; skip entirely.\n  - `[text]` followed by other text.\n\nSo for shorthand: look for `\\[(label)\\]` where the character after `]` is not `(` or `[`, and the line is not a definition line.\n\nBut wait — the full form `[text][label]`: if I scan the shorthand `\\[[^\\]]*\\]` with a negative lookahead `(?![\\(\\[])`, then in `[text][label]` the `[text]` is followed by `[` so it won't match as shorthand — good, and the full-form regex will match it. And `[label]` in `[text][label]` — could it match as shorthand? `[label]` at the end of a string is not followed by `(` or `[`, so it would match as shorthand and resolve `label` as a label. In CommonMark, `[text][label]` is parsed as a full reference link; the inner `[label]` isn't a separate link. So I need to avoid matching the inner label.\n\nApproach: do full-form and collapsed matching first, and exclude spans already consumed. Simplest: use a single combined regex for reference links with alternation, scan left-to-right, and handle consumed ranges.\n\nLet me define:\n\nREF_LINK_RE = re.compile(r\"\\[([^\\]]*)\\](?:\\[([^\\]]*)\\]|(?!\\[|\\()...\"))\n\nHmm, getting messy. Let me think again.\n\nCombined pattern for reference link candidates:\n- `\\[(text)\\]\\[label\\]` (full or collapsed)\n- `\\[(label)\\]` where the next char is not `(` or `[` (shorthand)\n\nCombined regex: `\\[(?P<text>[^\\]]*)\\](?:\\[(?P<label>[^\\]]*)\\]|(?!\\[|\\())/` — no, the negative lookahead needs to apply only to the shorthand alternative. In a Python regex: `\\[(?P<text>[^\\]]*)\\](?:\\[(?P<label>[^\\]]*)\\]|(?![\\(\\[)(?P=...))` — this doesn't quite work because alternatives: after the first `]`, either a second `[...]` follows, or a char that isn't `(` or `[` follows. So:\n\n`re.compile(r\"\\[([^\\]]*)\\](?:\\[([^\\]]*)\\]|(?![\\(\\[))\")`\n\nLet me verify: for `[text][label]`: match starts at `[text]`, then the group 2 branch matches `[label]`. Good. For `[text](target)`: after `[text]`, the alternative tries `\\[...\\]` — fails (next is `(`), then `(?![\\(\\[))` — fails because next is `(`. So no match at this position. Good — the inline link regex handles it. For `[label]` at end of line: after `[label]`, `(?![\\(\\[))` succeeds (no next char). Match with group 2 = None → shorthand, label = text. For `[text][label]` — could the match start at the inner `[label]`? After finditer finds the full match `[text][label]`, it continues after that. The inner `[label]` is consumed. Good.\n\nBut note: `[text][label]` where label is empty (collapsed): `[text][]` → group 2 = \"\" → use text as label.\n\nThen:\n- full/collapsed: label = group 2 if group 2 else group 1 (text)\n- shorthand: label = group 1 (text)\n\nActually both cases: label = group 2 or group 1.\n\nThen look up label.lower() in definitions (CommonMark: lab