{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Wait, I need to double-check the lookahead logic. The pattern for a reference link is:\n\n`\\[([^\\]]*)\\](?:\\[([^\\]]*)\\]|(?![\\(\\[))`\n\nSo it matches `[text]`, followed by either `[label]` or (lookahead: the next character is not `(` or `[`).\n\nFor `[text](target)` — after `[text]`, the next character is `(`, so the lookahead fails → no match. Good.\nFor `[text][label]` — after `[text]`, it matches `[label]`. Good.\nFor `[text]` at end of line or followed by a space — the lookahead succeeds. Good.\n\nBut wait, there's a subtle point: in the case of `[text][label]`, when finditer continues scanning, could it start a new match from `[label]`? No — finditer matches non-overlapping matches; after matching `[text][label]`, the scan continues from after `[label]`. Good.\n\nBut what about `[label]` on a definition line, e.g. `[idx]: index.md`? We skip the definition line entirely with `if REF_DEF_RE.match(line): continue`. Good.\n\nHmm, but wait: what about the case where a reference link uses a defined label, and the text portion of that link itself looks like a label… that's fine.\n\nAnother subtle point: in `[text][label]`, if the label itself is a defined label and the text isn't — no problem, the label is resolved via group 2.\n\nWhat about `[a][b]` where `[b]` is a defined label, and `[a]` is also defined? We take group 2 = `b`. Correct as CommonMark.\n\nWhat about `[a]` where `a` is defined → shorthand. Good.\n\nWhat about `[a][]`? group 2 = \"\" → falsy → use group 1 = `a`. Good.\n\nEdge case: an empty `[text]`? group 1 = \"\" → label \"\" → normalize → \"\" → probably not in defs → no yield. No problem.\n\nNow, the earlier `/tmp` issue: I'm inside a sandbox; mkdir /tmp/refcheck failed with \"Operation not permitted\". I'll create a scratch directory inside the working directory instead.\n\nLet me check where the working directory is.\n","seq":13120,"t":1978483,"text":"","timings":{"cache_n":19712,