{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Two scope decisions:\n\n1. **Completely remove `--check-remote`** — delete the argparse flag, the warning in main, and the docstring entry. Since the flag goes away, links with a scheme are still skipped (no change — the SCHEME_RE skip in check_file stays as-is, and the docstring says http/https are skipped).\n\n2. **Duplicate heading suffixes (-1, -2) like GitHub** — GitHub's rule: when a heading slug collides with a previous one, the first keeps the base slug, the second gets `-1`, the third gets `-2`, and so on. All headings in a file are processed in document order.\n\nImplementation: `collect_headings` currently returns a set of slugs. For duplicate handling, we need to return the *set of final slugs* after applying suffixes. Since multiple headings map to the same final slug (e.g., \"Foo\", \"Foo\", \"Foo\" → foo, foo-1, foo-2), a set of final slugs is exactly what we need for anchor checking: an anchor `foo-1` is valid iff some heading got that final slug.\n\nSo modify `collect_headings` to track occurrence counts:\n\n```python\ndef collect_headings(text):\n    \"\"\"Return the set of anchor slugs for ATX headings in text.\n\n    Duplicate headings get GitHub-style suffixes: the first \"Foo\"\n    keeps \"foo\", the second becomes \"foo-1\", the third \"foo-2\", etc.\n    \"\"\"\n    slugs = set()\n    counts = {}\n    in_fence = False\n    for line in text.splitlines():\n        if FENCE_RE.match(line):\n            in_fence = not in_fence\n            continue\n        if in_fence:\n            continue\n        m = ATX_RE.match(line)\n        if not m:\n            continue\n        heading = re.sub(r\"\\s+#+\\s*$\", \"\", m.group(1)).strip()\n        if not heading:\n            continue\n        base = heading_slug(heading)\n        n = counts.get(base, 0)\n        counts[base] = n + 1\n        slugs.add(base if n == 0 else f\"{base}-{n}\")\n    return slugs\n```\n\nThis is exactly GitHub's behavior: first occurrence → base, second → base-1, third → base-2.\n\nEdge case: what if the heading text itself is \"Foo-1\" and \"Foo\" appears twice? GitHub: Foo → foo, Foo-1 → foo-1 (no collision since it's a different heading text), Foo → foo-1... wait, collision! GitHub's algorithm: slug is computed per heading, and if it's already used, append -1, and if that's used, -2, etc. So:\n- \"Foo\" → foo\n- \"Foo-1\" → foo-1\n- \"Foo\" → foo is used → try foo-1 → used → try foo-2 → free → foo-2\n\nMy count-based approach: counts is keyed by base slug. \"Foo\" (n=0) → foo. \"Foo-1\" → base foo-1, n=0 → foo-1. \"Foo\" → base foo, n=1 → foo-1 — wrong! GitHub would give foo-2.\n\nTo do it properly, I need the \"try base, then base-1, base-2, ...\" algorithm:\n\n```python\ndef collect_headings(text):\n    slugs = set()\n    in_fence = False\n    for line in text.splitlines():\n        ...\n        base = heading_slug(heading)\n        slug = base\n        i = 1\n        while slug in slugs:\n            slug = f\"{base}-{i}\"\n            i += 1\n        slugs.add(slug)\n    return slugs\n```\n\nThis is the correct GitHub behavior, and handles the \"Foo\"/\"Foo-1\"/\"Foo\" case: foo, foo-1, foo-2.\n\nThis is O(n²) in the worst case, but fine for markdown files.\n\nNow, the anchor check in `check_file` uses `heading_slug(fragment) in headings` — the fragment \"foo-1\" slugifies to \"foo-1\" and is in the set. ✓\n\nNext, removing --check-remote:\n- Docstring: remove the `--check-remote` entry from the Options section, and update the \"Supported link forms\" bullet: \"``[text](https://.../foo.md)`` http/https checked only with ``--check-remote``; skipped by default\" → change to \"http/https links are skipped (local links only)\". Also the example line `python linkcheck.py --check-remote       # also verify http/https` — remove.\n- main: remove the `parser.add_argument(\"--check-remote\", ...)` and the warning block.\n- check_file comment: \"remote/mailto: not checked in this step\" → \"remote/mailto: out of scope (local links only)\".\n\nWait — should I also remove the `urllib.parse` import? No, it's still used for unquote.\n\nLet me check the current state of the file before editing. From the previous turn I know the file well. Let me re-read the relevant parts to be safe.\n\nAlso updating the followup memory entry: the followup listed unimplemented --check-remote, duplicate heading suffixes, and autolinks. Now: --check-remote is dropped (decided out of scope), duplicate suffixes implemented, autolinks still unimplemented. I should record the new decision and update the followup.\n\nTests:\n1. Duplicate headings in a scratch file: `# Foo`, `# Foo`, `# Foo-1`, `# Foo` → slugs are foo, foo-1, foo-2, foo-3. Links to #foo, #foo-1, #foo-2, #foo-3 are ok; #foo-4 is noanchor.\n2. Verify that removing the flag works: `python3 linkcheck.py --check-remote` → exit 2 (unrecognized argument).\n3. Regression test with docs/: same 3 broken links as before.\n\nShould I add duplicate headings to docs? The user said \"implement GitHub-style duplicate heading suffixes (-1, -2)\" — testing with docs/ is implicit from the earlier pattern (\"test both with docs/\" was last time). This time the user didn't explicitly say to test with docs. I'll test with a scratch file for the suffix behavior and run a regression with docs/. It might be nice to add a duplicate heading to docs/beta.md to demo it — hmm, adding a duplicate heading changes the existing anchor semantics: beta.md has \"# Beta\", \"## Useful Section\", \"## Notes\". If I add a duplicate \"## Notes\", the second becomes notes-1. Existing links to #useful-section are unaffected. But do I want to modify docs for this? The user's instruction is focused on the tool. I'll do scratch testing for the suffix logic and a regression run with docs/. Actually, let me add a small duplicate-heading case to docs so the feature is actually exercised in the fixture set — that was the pattern last time (adding a broken ref to alpha.md). I think adding one duplicate heading with a link to the suffixed anchor is worth it. Let me add to beta.md:\n\n```\n## Notes\n\nBeta also links to a [nonexistent anchor](beta.md#no-such-heading).\n\n## Notes\n\nA duplicate heading gets the -1 suffix: [duplicate notes](beta.md#notes-1).\n```\n\nSo beta.md has two \"## Notes\" → slugs are notes, notes-1. Link to #notes-1 → ok. And #notes → still ok (first). Good, that's a valid case. Should I also test a broken suffixed anchor? #notes-2 would be noanchor. Hmm, I want to keep docs as the source of truth for the expected broken set: currently 3 broken. Adding #notes-2 as a broken anchor would make it 4. That's fine — it demonstrates the suffix. Let me add:\n\nActually, simpler: one duplicate heading, one valid link to the suffixed anchor. The broken-anchor case is already covered by the existing #no-such-heading. Suffix edge cases (Foo/Foo-1/Foo) I'll verify in scratch.\n\nLet me write the edits.\n\nFirst, read the current file to get the exact text.\n","seq":14257,"t":2060427,"text":"","timings":{"cache_n":24320,"draft_n":3780,"draft_n_accepted":1318,"predicted_ms":21980.0,"predicted_n":1858,"prompt_ms":1910.0,"prompt_n":1692},"to_request":13718,"usage":{"cached_tokens":24320,"completion_tokens":1858,"prompt_tokens":26012}}
{"arguments":"{\"path\": \"linkcheck.py\"}","id":"call_00f1b4c17bba4bec9473376b","kind":"tool_call","name":"read","outcome":"ran","request":13718,"seq":14258,"shown":"\"\"\"Markdown link checker.\n\nScans Markdown files for internal links and verifies that the targets\nexist. Standard library only; no third-party dependencies.\n\nSupported link forms\n--------------------\n- ``[text](path/to/file.md)``      relative file links\n- ``[text](dir/file.md#anchor)``   anchor checked against ATX headings\n- ``[text][ref]``                  reference-style links, resolved via\n  ``[ref]: target`` definitions in the same file; the shorthand\n  ``[ref]`` and collapsed ``[text][]`` forms work too. Definitions\n  themselves are not reported — only the links that use them\n- ``[text](https://.../foo.md)``   http/https checked only with\n  ``--check-remote``; skipped by default\n- Image links ``![alt](img.png)``  checked as file targets\n\nMissing targets are reported as ``file:line: target``; existing\ntargets whose anchor cannot be found are reported as\n``file:line: target (NOANCHOR)``.\n\nUsage\n-----\n    python linkcheck.py [FILES ...] [OPTIONS]\n\nWith no FILES, the current directory is scanned recursively for\n``*.md`` files (skipping ``.git``, ``node_modules``, ``vendor``).\n\nExamples\n--------\n    python linkcheck.py                      # all *.md in this tree\n    python linkcheck.py README.md docs/      # specific files/dirs\n    python linkcheck.py --check-remote       # also verify http/https\n    python linkcheck.py --allow-missing      # missing targets exit 0\n\nOptions\n-------\n    --check-remote    Also verify http/https links (HEAD request)\n    --allow-missing   Report missing targets but exit 0\n    --base-dir DIR    Resolve relative links against DIR (default:\n                      the directory of each file)\n    --json            Print broken links as a JSON list instead of lines\n    --verbose         One line per link checked, not just failures\n\nExit codes\n----------\n    0  all links OK (or only warnings, with --allow-missing)\n    1  one or more missing targets / failed remote checks\n    2  usage error (bad arguments, no files found)\n\"\"\"\n\nimport argparse\nimport json\nimport os\nimport re\nimport sys\nimport urllib.parse\n\nSKIP_DIRS = {\".git\", \"node_modules\", \"vendor\"}\n\n# Inline link [text](target); also matches image links ![alt](target).\nLINK_RE = re.compile(r\"\\[[^\\]]*\\]\\(([^()\\s]+)(?:\\s+\\\"[^\\\"]*\\\")?\\)\")\n# Reference definition line: [label]: destination \"optional title\".\nREF_DEF_RE = re.compile(\n    r\"^\\s{0,3}\\[([^\\]]+)\\]:\\s*(<[^>\\n]*>|\\S+)(?:\\s+\\\"[^\\\"]*\\\")?\\s*$\"\n)\n# Reference link: [text][label], [text][] (collapsed), or [label]\n# (shorthand). The lookahead keeps [text](...) inline links out of the\n# shorthand form.\nREF_LINK_RE = re.compile(r\"\\[([^\\]]*)\\](?:\\[([^\\]]*)\\]|(?![\\(\\[]))\")\nFENCE_RE = re.compile(r\"^\\s*(```|~~~)\")\nSCHEME_RE = re.compile(r\"^[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9+.\\-]*:\")\nATX_RE = re.compile(r\"^#{1,6}\\s+(.*)$\")\n\n\ndef find_md_files(paths):\n    \"\"\"Collect .md files from the given files/dirs (recursively).\n\n    Returns a sorted list, or None if a path does not exist.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not paths:\n        paths = [\".\"]\n    files = []\n    for p in paths:\n        if os.path.isfile(p):\n            files.append(p)\n        elif os.path.isdir(p):\n            for root, dirs, names in os.walk(p):\n                dirs[:] = sorted(d for d in dirs if d not in SKIP_DIRS)\n                for name in sorted(names):\n                    if name.lower().endswith(\".md\"):\n                        files.append(os.path.normpath(os.path.join(root, name)))\n        else:\n            print(f\"error: {p}: no such file or directory\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return None\n    return sorted(set(files))\n\n\ndef normalize_label(label):\n    \"\"\"Normalize a reference label: case-insensitive, whitespace collapsed.\"\"\"\n    return re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", label.strip()).lower()\n\n\ndef parse_ref_definitions(text):\n    \"\"\"Collect reference definitions from text.\n\n    Returns a dict mapping normalized label to target, skipping code\n    fences. A definition line is ``[label]: destination`` with an\n    optional ``\"title\"``; ``<...>`` wrappers around the destination\n    are stripped.\n    \"\"\"\n    defs = {}\n    in_fence = False\n    for line in text.splitlines():\n        if FENCE_RE.match(line):\n            in_fence = not in_fence\n            continue\n        if in_fence:\n            continue\n        m = REF_DEF_RE.match(line)\n        if not m:\n            continue\n        target = m.group(2)\n        if target.startswith(\"<\") and target.endswith(\">\"):\n            target = target[1:-1]\n        defs[normalize_label(m.group(1))] = target\n    return defs\n\n\ndef extract_links(text, ref_defs=None):\n    \"\"\"Yield (line_number, target) for inline and reference links.\n\n    Skips code fences and definition lines; definition targets are\n    checked through the links that reference them.\n    \"\"\"\n    ref_defs = ref_defs or {}\n    in_fence = False\n    for lineno, line in enumerate(text.splitlines(), 1):\n        if FENCE_RE.match(line):\n            in_fence = not in_fence\n            continue\n        if in_fence:\n            continue\n        if REF_DEF_RE.match(line):\n            continue\n        for m in LINK_RE.finditer(line):\n            yield lineno, m.group(1)\n        for m in REF_LINK_RE.finditer(line):\n            label = m.group(2) if m.group(2) else m.group(1)\n            target = ref_defs.get(normalize_label(label))\n            if target is not None:\n                yield lineno, target\n\n\ndef collect_headings(text):\n    \"\"\"Return the set of GitHub-style slugs for ATX headings in text.\"\"\"\n    slugs = set()\n    in_fence = False\n    for line in text.splitlines():\n        if FENCE_RE.match(line):\n            in_fence = not in_fence\n            continue\n        if in_fence:\n            continue\n        m = ATX_RE.match(line)\n        if not m:\n            continue\n        heading = re.sub(r\"\\s+#+\\s*$\", \"\", m.group(1)).strip()\n        if heading:\n            slugs.add(heading_slug(heading))\n    return slugs\n\n\ndef check_file(path, base_dir):\n    \"\"\"Check one file.\n\n    Returns a list of (line_number, target, status) for local links,\n    where status is \"ok\", \"missing\", or \"noanchor\"; or None if the\n    file cannot be read.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        with open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n            text = f.read()\n    except OSError as e:\n        print(f\"error: {path}: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return None\n\n    ref_defs = parse_ref_definitions(text)\n    base = base_dir or os.path.dirname(path)\n    results = []\n    for lineno, target in extract_links(text, ref_defs):\n        if SCHEME_RE.match(target):\n            continue  # remote/mailto: not checked in this step\n        path_part, _, fragment = target.partition(\"#\")\n        path_part = urllib.parse.unquote(path_part)\n        fragment = urllib.parse.unquote(fragment)\n        target_path = path if not path_part else os.path.join(base, path_part)\n        if not os.path.exists(target_path):\n            results.append((lineno, target, \"missing\"))\n            continue\n        if not fragment:\n            results.append((lineno, target, \"ok\"))\n            continue\n        try:\n            with open(target_path, encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n                headings = collect_headings(f.read())\n        except OSError:\n            results.append((lineno, target, \"ok\"))  # skip anchor check\n            continue\n        status = \"ok\" if heading_slug(fragment) in headings else \"noanchor\"\n        results.append((lineno, target, status))\n    return results\n\n\ndef main(argv=None):\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        prog=\"linkcheck\", description=\"Check local links in Markdown files.\"\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\"files\", nargs=\"*\", help=\"Markdown files or directories\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--base-dir\", help=\"resolve relative links against DIR\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--allow-missing\", action=\"store_true\",\n                        help=\"report missing targets but exit 0\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--check-remote\", action=\"store_true\",\n                        help=\"verify http/https links (reserved for a later step)\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--verbose\", action=\"store_true\",\n                        help=\"print a line for every link checked\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--json\", action=\"store_true\",\n                        help=\"print broken links as a JSON list instead of lines\")\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n\n    if args.check_remote:\n        print(\"warning: --check-remote is not implemented yet; \"\n              \"remote links are skipped\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n    files = find_md_files(args.files)\n    if files is None:\n        return 2\n    if not files:\n        print(\"error: no .md files found\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n\n    broken = []\n    for path in files:\n        results = check_file(path, args.base_dir)\n        if results is None:\n            return 2\n        for lineno, target, status in results:\n            if status == \"ok\":\n                if args.verbose:\n                    print(f\"{path}:{lineno}: {target} (ok)\")\n            else:\n                broken.append((path, lineno, target, status))\n\n    if args.json:\n        payload = [\n            {\"file\": path, \"line\": lineno, \"target\": target, \"status\": status}\n            for path, lineno, target, status in broken\n        ]\n        print(json.dumps(payload, indent=2))\n    else:\n        for path, lineno, target, status in broken:\n            suffix = \" (NOANCHOR)\" if status == \"noanchor\" else \"\"\n            print(f\"{path}:{lineno}: {target}{suffix}\")\n    return 1 if broken and not args.allow_missing else 0\n\n\ndef heading_slug(text):\n    \"\"\"Turn a heading into a GitHub-style anchor slug.\n\n    Example: \"Hello, World!\" -> \"hello-world\".\n    \"\"\"\n    slug = text.strip().lower()\n    slug = re.sub(r\"[^\\w\\s-]\", \"\", slug)\n    slug = re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \"-\", slug)\n    return slug\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())","t":2060453,"turn":8}
