{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"Recorded. The follow-up entry is partially resolved — duplicate heading suffixes are complete, --check-remote has been removed (no longer a follow-up), and autolinks remain unimplemented. The resolve_entry call handles the status. Done — wrapping up.\n","seq":14816,"t":2108387,"text":"\n\nBoth scope decisions are in.\n\n**`--check-remote` dropped**\n- Removed the argparse flag, the stderr warning, and all docstring mentions (options, example, the http/https bullet — now just says remote links are skipped, local-only). `SCHEME_RE` skip in `check_file` stays; `--check-remote` now yields a usage error, exit 2.\n\n**Duplicate-heading suffixes** — `collect_headings` now implements GitHub's collision algorithm: for each heading in document order, take the base slug, and if it's already taken try `base-1`, `base-2`, … until free. The set of final slugs is what anchor checking consults, so no other code changed. Verified the tricky case where the count-based shortcut would fail: `Foo`, `Foo-1`, `Foo` → `foo`, `foo-1`, `foo-2` (the third `Foo` skips `foo-1` because the `Foo-1` heading holds it); `Bar` ×3 → `bar`, `bar-1`, `bar-2`.\n\n**Tested:**\n- Scratch fixture (deleted after): `#foo`, `#foo-1`, `#foo-2` → `(ok)`; `#foo-3`, `#bar-3` → `(NOANCHOR)`; exit 1\n- `docs/beta.md` gained a duplicate `## Notes` with links to `#notes` and `#notes-1` — both `(ok)` under `--verbose`\n- Regression: `python3 linkcheck.py docs` still reports exactly the 3 known broken links (`alpha.md:6 missing.md`, `beta.md:3 missing.md`, `beta.md:11 NOANCHOR`), exit 1; `--json` output unchanged; `--check-remote` → exit 2\n\nWhat I'd test if extending: Setext headings (`Heading\\n===`) and heading slugs containing characters GitHub treats differently (e.g. `_` vs `-`) — both outside the current ATX-only scope.","timings":{"cache_n":34560,"draft_n":1008,"draft_n_accepted":355,"predicted_ms":6130.0,"predicted_n":499,"prompt_ms":1160.0,"prompt_n":685}