{"items":[{"key":"seam-8/message-50","score":2,"sha256":"930718ed851d796bb33953fa73b17f574576b998bb98a90862a1b62569f3b46a"},{"key":"seam-8/message-46","score":2,"sha256":"7dd06a37e5531ca8a9a328a5037d8da4dce194a7530eb96aacd5b5bccde9b4b5"},{"key":"seam-8/message-45","score":2,"sha256":"5f522b25da7051357e065e9ffb1456025dff09fef75f5e1b592303bab93e02e5"}],"kind":"recalled","recall":"literal","seq":15011,"t":2146637,"text":"<system-reminder>\n## Relevant memory\n\nUse the following memories only when they are directly relevant to the current request. Verify file/function claims before relying on them.\n\n### a tool result (seam-8/message-50)\n\nThe file: linkcheck.py has been updated. Showing lines 150-184 of 289 from the edited file:\n\n---\n\n                yield lineno, target\n\n\ndef collect_headings(text):\n    \"\"\"Return the set of anchor slugs for ATX headings in text.\n\n    Duplicates get GitHub-style suffixes in document order: the first\n    \"Foo\" keeps \"foo\", the second becomes \"foo-1\", the third \"foo-2\",\n    and so on (a suffix number is skipped if a heading already holds\n    it, e.g. \"Foo\", \"Foo-1\", \"Foo\" -> foo, foo-1, foo-2).\n    \"\"\"\n    slugs = set()\n    in_fence = False\n    for line in text.splitlines():\n        if FENCE_RE.match(line):\n            in_fence = not in_fence\n            continue\n        if in_fence:\n            continue\n        m = ATX_RE.match(line)\n        if not m:\n            continue\n        heading = re.sub(r\"\\s+#+\\s*$\", \"\", m.group(1)).strip()\n        if not heading:\n            continue\n        base = heading_slug(heading)\n        slug, i = base, 1\n        while slug in slugs:\n            slug = f\"{base}-{i}\"\n            i += 1\n        slugs.add(slug)\n    return slugs\n\n\ndef check_file(path, base_dir):\n\n### a step that called tools (seam-8/message-46)\n\nMaking the changes: drop `--check-remote` (flag, warning, docstring), and give `collect_headings` GitHub's \"try `base`, then `base-1`, `base-2`…\" collision algorithm.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nedit {\"file_path\": \"linkcheck.py\", \"old_string\": \"- ``[text](https://.../foo.md)``   http/https checked only with\\n  ``--check-remote``; skipped by default\\n- Image links ``![alt](img.png)``  checked as file targets\", \"new_string\": \"- ``[text](https://.../foo.md)``   http/https links are skipped\\n  (local links only); mailto: likewise\\n- Image links ``![alt](img.png)``  checked as file targets\"}\nedit {\"file_path\": \"linkcheck.py\", \"old_string\": \"python linkcheck.py                      # all *.md in this tree\\n    python linkcheck.py README.md docs/      # specific files/dirs\\n    python linkcheck.py --check-remote       # also verify http/https\\n    python linkcheck.py --allow-missing      # missing targets exit 0\\n\\nOptions\\n-------\\n    --check-remote    Also verify http/https links (HEAD request)\\n    --allow-missing   Report missing targets but exit 0\", \"new_string\": \"python linkcheck.py                      # all *.md in this tree\\n    python linkcheck.py README.md docs/      # specific files/dirs\\n    py […]\n\n> NOTE: Relevant memory truncated for prompt budget.\n\n### a tool result (seam-8/message-45)\n\n[…] https checked only with\n  ``--check-remote``; skipped by default\n- Image links ``![alt](img.png)``  checked as file targets\n\nMissing targets are reported as ``file:line: target``; existing\ntargets whose anchor cannot be found are reported as\n``file:line: target (NOANCHOR)``.\n\nUsage\n-----\n    python linkcheck.py [FILES ...] [OPTIONS]\n\nWith no FILES, the current directory is scanned recursively for\n``*.md`` files (skipping ``.git``, ``node_modules``, ``vendor``).\n\nExamples\n--------\n    python linkcheck.py                      # all *.md in this tree\n    python linkcheck.py README.md docs/      # specific files/dirs\n    python linkcheck.py --check-remote       # also verify http/https\n    python linkcheck.py --allow-missing      # missing targets exit 0\n\nOptions\n-------\n    --check-remote    Also verify http/https links (HEAD request)\n    --allow-missing   Report missing targets but exit 0\n    --base-dir DIR    Resolve relative links against DIR (default:\n                      the directory of each file)\n    --json            Print broken links as a JSON list instead of lines\n    --verbose         One line per link checked, not just failures\n\nExit codes\n----------\n    0  all links OK […]\n\n> NOTE: Relevant memory truncated for prompt budget.\n\n</system-reminder>","turn":9}
